      "body": "## Motivation\n\n`RMSNorm(has_weight=False)` stores its all-ones weight as a plain tensor attribute:\n\n```python\nself.weight = torch.ones(hidden_size, dtype=weight_dtype)\n```\n\nIt is neither a parameter nor a buffer, but it is allocated in the weights memory region. `release_memory_occupation` with the `weights` tag frees that region, and `resume_memory_occupation` restores only what it knows about: weight updates refill the parameters, and `_export_static_state` / `_import_static_state` refill the buffers. This tensor is neither, so after a release and resume it comes back as zeros.\n\nFor a model that normalizes its token embeddings with a weightless `RMSNorm`, the hidden states are then multiplied by zero, and every logit is equal: generation returns a uniform distribution over the vocabulary and sampling fails with `Sampling support exceeds --sampling-mask-max-tokens`. We hit this with Muse Glimmer (`embed_norm` and the per-layer `qk_norm` both use `has_weight=False`) when offloading the rollout engine between RL steps. A standalone SGLang server on one GPU reproduces it: greedy output before `release_memory_occupation(tags=[\"weights\"])` / `resume_memory_occupation(tags=[\"weights\"])` is correct; after, every top log-probability is exactly `-log(vocab_size)`. Releasing only the KV cache does not trigger it, and `--enable-weights-cpu-backup` hides it.\n\n## Modifications\n\nRegister the weightless weight as a non-persistent buffer instead of a plain attribute. It then takes part in the existing buffer snapshot across release and resume, still follows `.to()` dtype and device moves, and stays out of `state_dict()`, so checkpoint loading is unchanged.\n\nAdds `test/registered/unit/layers/test_rmsnorm_weightless_buffer.py`, which fails without the change.\n\n## Accuracy Tests\n\nNo change to model outputs in normal serving: the buffer holds the same ones. With the change, outputs after a weights release and resume match the outputs before it.\n\n## Speed Tests and Profiling\n\nNo impact: the same tensor, registered differently.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Format your code according to the [Format code with pre-commit](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#format-code-with-pre-commit).\n- [x] Add unit tests according to the [Run and add unit tests](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#run-and-add-unit-tests).\n- [ ] Update documentation according to [Write documentations](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#write-documentations).\n- [ ] Provide accuracy and speed benchmark results according to [Test the accuracy](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#test-the-accuracy) and [Benchmark the speed](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#benchmark-the-speed).\n- [x] Follow the SGLang code style [guidance](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#code-style-guidance).\n\n<!-- pr-states:start -->\n---\n### CI States\n\nLatest PR Test (Base): <!-- slot:pr-test:start -->:x: [Run #40779350655](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/40779350655)<!-- slot:pr-test:end -->\nLatest PR Test (Extra): <!-- slot:pr-test-extra:start -->:x: [Run #40779350524](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/40779350524)<!-- slot:pr-test-extra:end -->\nLatest PR Test (AMD ROCm 10): <!-- slot:pr-test-amd:start -->:x: [Run #40779350589](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/40779350589)<!-- slot:pr-test-amd:end -->\n<!-- pr-states:end -->",
